{"text": " You know, what I do is write for children. I'm probably America's most widely read children's author, in fact, and I always tell people that I don't want to show up looking like a scientist. You can have me as a farmer or in leathers, and no one has ever chose farmer. I'm here today to talk to you about circles and epiphanies, and you know, an epiphany is usually something you find that you drop someplace. You just got to go around the block to see it as an epiphany. That's a painting of a circle, a friend of mine did that, Richard Ballingbroke. It's the kind of complicated circle that I'm going to tell you about. My circle began back in the 60s in high school in Stowe, Ohio, where I was the class queer. I was the guy beaten up bloody every week in the boys' room until one teacher saved my life. She saved my life by letting me go to the bathroom in the teacher's lounge. She did it in secret, she did it for three years, and I had to get out of town. I had a thumb, I had $85, and I ended up in San Francisco, California, met a lover, and back in the 80s found it necessary to begin work on AIDS organizations. About three or four years ago, I got a phone call in the middle of the night from that teacher, Mrs. Poston, who said, I need to see you. I'm disappointed that we never got to know each other as adults. Would you please come to Ohio and please bring that man that I know you have found by now? And I should mention that I have pancreatic cancer, and I'd like you to please be quick about this. Well, the next day we were in Cleveland. We took a look at her, we laughed, we cried, and we knew that she needed to be in a hospice. We found her one, we got her there, and we took care of her and watched over her family because it was necessary, it's something we knew how to do. And just as a woman who wanted to know me as an adult got to know me, she turned into a box of ashes and was placed in my hand. And what had happened was the circle had closed, it had become a circle. And that epiphany I talked about presented itself. The epiphany is that death is a part of life. She saved my life, I and my partner saved hers. And you know, that part of life needs everything that the rest of life does. It needs truth and beauty, and I'm so happy it's been mentioned so much here today. It also needs dignity, love, and pleasure, and it's our job to hand those things out. Thank you. Thank you. Thank you. Thank you. Thank you. Thank you. Thank you. Thank you.", "segments": [{"id": 0, "seek": 0, "start": 0.0, "end": 18.68, "text": " You know, what I do is write for children.", "tokens": [50363, 921, 760, 11, 644, 314, 466, 318, 3551, 329, 1751, 13, 51297], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.19062326504633978, "compression_ratio": 1.3475177304964538, "no_speech_prob": 0.060222264379262924}, {"id": 1, "seek": 0, "start": 18.68, "end": 24.76, "text": " I'm probably America's most widely read children's author, in fact, and I always tell people", "tokens": [51297, 314, 1101, 2192, 2253, 338, 749, 6768, 1100, 1751, 338, 1772, 11, 287, 1109, 11, 290, 314, 1464, 1560, 661, 51601], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.19062326504633978, "compression_ratio": 1.3475177304964538, "no_speech_prob": 0.060222264379262924}, {"id": 2, "seek": 0, "start": 24.76, "end": 28.060000000000002, "text": " that I don't want to show up looking like a scientist.", "tokens": [51601, 326, 314, 836, 470, 765, 284, 905, 510, 2045, 588, 257, 11444, 13, 51766], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.19062326504633978, "compression_ratio": 1.3475177304964538, "no_speech_prob": 0.060222264379262924}, {"id": 3, "seek": 2806, "start": 28.06, "end": 34.96, "text": " You can have me as a farmer or in leathers, and no one has ever chose farmer.", "tokens": [50363, 921, 460, 423, 502, 355, 257, 18739, 393, 287, 11620, 82, 11, 290, 645, 530, 468, 1683, 7690, 18739, 13, 50708], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13729071814166613, "compression_ratio": 1.6929133858267718, "no_s